# ── Sign-in ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# There is no credential in this file. FabOrchestrator is the only identity:
# every visitor signs in with their own FO account, which FO verifies against
# its own user table, and the app holds only the session that results.
#
# A local DEMO_USER_* pair used to be accepted here. It was removed on
# 2026-09-01 (WP2) because it minted a session with no FabOrchestrator token —
# one that reached the app, opened an agent, and could not ask a question. Set
# FABORCH_BASE_URL below; that is what makes sign-in work.

# Secret the session token is signed with. Any long random string.
SESSION_SIGNING_SECRET=replace-with-a-long-random-string

# How long a signed-in session lasts, in hours.
SESSION_TTL_HOURS=12

# Tier 1 — the Explain Issue flow. Without a key the app still works: every
# Explain falls back to the cached analysis for that order and says so on the
# card. Orders with no cached analysis return an honest 503.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Optional. Defaults to claude-haiku-4-5-20251001 and 40000 ms.
#
# The model is handed the facts, the fired issues and the exact records it may
# cite, then held to a strict schema and a grounding check — a narrow job that
# does not need a frontier model. If explanation quality drops, switch to
# claude-opus-5 here; no code change is needed.
#
# The timeout is PER ATTEMPT, and a corrective retry costs two. It was raised
# from 25000 after the first live calls came back at 21.1 s and 19.1 s on
# Opus 5. Haiku 4.5 is roughly half that.
#
# Note: Haiku 4.5 rejects `output_config.effort` with a 400. lib/ai/analyze.ts
# only sends that parameter to models that accept it.
# ANALYSIS_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# ANALYSIS_TIMEOUT_MS=40000

# ── Tier 3 — the decision log ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unset, decisions live in a per-process Map: they empty on restart, on every
# hot reload and on every redeploy, and both screens say `durable: false` out
# loud. That is the right default for `npm run dev` and for the test suites,
# which should not need a database to run.
#
# Set, decisions go to Postgres and `durable` becomes true. The table is created
# on first use (`CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`), so there is no migration step to
# forget — point this at an empty database and it works.
#
# It is INSERT-ONLY. An override writes a new row carrying supersedes_id; the
# original is never rewritten, which is the one property an audit trail exists
# to have.
#
# Any managed Postgres works — Neon, Supabase and Railway all hand you a URL in
# this shape. TLS certificate verification is skipped (the connection is still
# encrypted), because those providers terminate TLS with roots a slim container
# does not carry.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/dbname?sslmode=require

# ── FabInsight — the connection to the real FabOrchestrator ───────────────────
#
# FabInsight is not built here. It is FabOrchestrator's own agent — AGENT · 01
# on the product's cockpit — and this PWA is a client for it: `/fabinsight`
# forwards the prompt to the running FO application, FO's agent and its approved
# tools answer it, and the answer is streamed back and rendered. Nothing about
# yield, downtime or defects is computed in this app for that screen.
#
# Point this at the FabOrchestrator you want answers from. Locally that is
# `claudeai_athena` (`npm run dev` in a clone of the FabOrchestrator product
# repo, `LLM-AT-SCALE/FabOrchestrator_product_code`, which serves :3000 and
# needs its own DATABASE_URL and ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).
#
# **Required.** Since the demo credential was removed, this is the only way to
# sign in at all: unset, the login route answers 503 and says so, rather than
# telling a visitor their password is wrong.
#
# There is no default on purpose. A localhost fallback would mean a deployment
# that forgot this setting silently answered from whatever happened to be on
# :3000, and the failure would look like strange answers rather than a missing
# setting.
#
# ── MUST be https, except on localhost ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Sign-in sends the operator's FabOrchestrator PASSWORD over this connection,
# and every call after it carries their session token; the questions and the
# plant data come back the same way. Over plain http all of it is readable by
# anything on the path.
#
# `foBaseUrl()` enforces this and there is no override:
#
#   https://anything                 accepted
#   http://localhost:3000            accepted — a FabOrchestrator on this machine
#   http://127.0.0.1:3000            accepted
#   http://anything-else             REFUSED, with a message saying why
#
# The exception is keyed on the host, not on NODE_ENV — `npm start` runs an
# ordinary local build with NODE_ENV=production, and a loopback address is
# unreachable from elsewhere by construction, which is the stronger guarantee.
# Note `http://localhost.example.com` is a REMOTE host and is refused.
#
# Production (fly.toml) points at https://d7y8a8whrch88.cloudfront.net.
# CloudFront terminates TLS; the Elastic Beanstalk origin behind it also answers
# on plain http, and pointing at that directly is exactly what this guard stops.
#
FABORCH_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional. Which model FabOrchestrator should answer with.
#
# Defaults to `claude-opus-4-8` — FO's own default, which it presents as
# "FabOrchestrator 2.0" (`GET /api/chat`). FO validates the id against its
# `model_registry` table and against the signed-in user's role, so an id that
# deployment does not allow comes back as an error rather than a silent
# substitution. Only set this if a deployment allows a different set.
#
# FABORCH_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# ── Signing in ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One credential: a FabOrchestrator account. It opens the agents *and* the
# production order workflow, which runs on this app's mock MES.
#
# The FO session token is held in an httpOnly cookie and never reaches client
# JavaScript. There is no service account and no shared FO identity: a demo is
# not a reason to hand whoever holds the URL somebody else's MES access.
